; ct_wave_i2s.pio -- PIO I2S MASTER transport for 2x UDA1334A stereo DACs.
; See ct_wave_i2s.h/.c for the full design rationale (GPIO conflicts, DMA
; mutual exclusion, PIO1 program-memory conflict, clkdiv arithmetic) -- this
; file only carries the bit-level derivation for the program itself.
;
; ONE PROGRAM, TWO SM INSTANCES (module A and module B) -- see ct_wave_i2s.c
; header's "two SMs, one program, no double-driven pins" note for why, and
; for how module B's SM ends up NOT actually driving BCLK/WS despite running
; the identical instruction stream.
;
; --- Pin-mapping contract (enforced by ct_wave_i2s.c's sm_config_set_*_pins
; calls, not by pioasm) ---
;   side-set (2 bits): base = BCLK gpio, base+1 = WS gpio. PIO side-set pins
;   are always a contiguous block starting at a configured base -- this is
;   why BCLK/WS must be adjacent ascending GPIOs (ct_wave_i2s.c's
;   _Static_assert enforces it, the same rule family as docs/HARDWARE.md
;   section 2's IN_BASE/OUT_BASE-offset constraints for the SPI programs).
;   Side value convention used throughout this file: 0bWB (bit1 = WS,
;   bit0 = BCLK).
;   OUT_BASE = this instance's DATA gpio (DIN_A for module A's SM, DIN_B for
;   module B's) -- `out pins, 1` drives exactly that one pin.
;
; --- Shift contract (matches ct_wave_i2s.c's sm_config_set_out_shift) ---
;   shift_left, autopull, threshold = 16. Each autopull refills the OSR from
;   ONE int16 sample -- not a packed 32-bit L+R word. This is deliberate:
;   the caller's buffers are plain interleaved-int16 stereo PCM (this
;   driver's seam contract), and the RP2040 PIO TX FIFO hardware positions a
;   16-bit-wide bus write into the correct (most-significant) shift position
;   automatically (RP2040 datasheet, PIO chapter, byte/halfword FIFO access
;   note) -- so ct_wave_i2s.c's DMA channel can move the caller's buffer
;   straight into txf[sm] with DMA_SIZE_16 and zero CPU repacking. Consuming
;   one 16-bit sample per autopull, in buffer order (L, R, L, R, ...), lines
;   up exactly with this program's own left-word/right-word alternation
;   below -- no software byte-swapping or word assembly needed anywhere.
;
; --- Timing derivation (I2S / Philips spec, matches the NXP UDA1334ATS
; datasheet's serial-data-input section) -----------------------------------
; DATA changes on BCLK's FALLING edge and is sampled by the DAC on BCLK's
; RISING edge -- a full half-period of setup/hold margin, not a razor's-edge
; race. WS (LRCLK) is LOW while the LEFT word's bits are on the wire, HIGH
; during RIGHT's, but the transition happens exactly 1 BCLK cycle *before*
; the new channel's first (MSB) bit -- i.e. the WS edge lands on the SAME
; falling edge that carries the OUTGOING word's very last (LSB) bit. This is
; "one BCLK delay after the WS edge" (ct_wave_i2s.h's header), worked out
; here bit-by-bit rather than left as a citation:
;
;   cycle (BCLK edge):      action                              WS
;   ------------------------------------------------------------------
;   left bit15  (falling):  out -- data changes                  0
;   left bit15  (rising):   sample point                         0
;   ...  (bits 14..1, same pattern, 14 more times)                0
;   left bit0   (falling):  out -- LSB of LEFT word               1  <- flips HERE, one cycle early
;   left bit0   (rising):   sample point                          1
;   right bit15 (falling):  out -- MSB of RIGHT word              1
;   ...  (bits 14..1, same pattern, 14 more times)                 1
;   right bit0  (falling):  out -- LSB of RIGHT word               0  <- flips HERE, anticipating the next LEFT word
;   right bit0  (rising):   sample point                           0
;   [wraps back to left bit15 of the next frame, WS already 0]
;
; Each bit costs exactly 2 SM instruction-cycles: one falling-edge
; instruction that also does the "out" (data changes exactly as BCLK goes
; low, so it is fully settled before the next rising edge), and one
; rising-edge instruction that is either the loop's "jmp" or, on each word's
; LAST bit, a "set x, 14" that does double duty -- it IS that bit's
; rising/sample-point instruction (BCLK still has to toggle every
; instruction regardless of whether that instruction also shifts data) AND
; it reloads the bit counter for the NEXT word, at zero extra cycle cost.
;
; X holds the remaining-bit counter through `jmp x--`, preloaded to 14 by
; ct_wave_i2s.c's pio_sm_exec(pio_encode_set(pio_x, 14)) before the SM is
; first enabled -- the "set x, 14" instructions inside the program only
; re-arm it for the FOLLOWING word, so the very first word out of reset
; needs it seeded externally once.
.program ct_wave_i2s_out
.side_set 2
.wrap_target
left_bit:
    out pins, 1        side 0b00   ; falling edge, WS=0 (left) -- bits 15..1 (15 passes total via the jmp loop below)
    jmp x--, left_bit   side 0b01  ; rising edge / sample point, WS=0
    out pins, 1         side 0b10  ; falling edge -- LEFT bit0 (LSB); WS flips to 1 now, anticipating RIGHT
    set x, 14            side 0b11 ; rising edge / sample point for LEFT's LSB (WS=1); also reloads the counter for the RIGHT word -- free, see header
right_bit:
    out pins, 1          side 0b10  ; falling edge, WS=1 (right) -- bits 15..1
    jmp x--, right_bit    side 0b11 ; rising edge / sample point, WS=1
    out pins, 1           side 0b00 ; falling edge -- RIGHT bit0 (LSB); WS flips to 0 now, anticipating the NEXT LEFT word
    set x, 14              side 0b01 ; rising edge / sample point for RIGHT's LSB (WS=0); reloads the counter for the next LEFT word
.wrap
